SM(513,{"n":"Romans 8","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 8.mp3","d":2492.1,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[23.52,"Start recording. Okay."],[26.4,"So when we get started here in just a minute, we're gonna be picking up in uh Romans chapter 8."],[33.04,"Uh I know it's been like a couple of months since we had a Bible study."],[38.64,"And I'm not even sure who here was there."],[41.12,"In fact, some of you I know weren't here two months ago."],[45.44,"So uh by way of catch-up and sort of reintroduction, and by the time, as I usually say, by the time this is uh on Ancient Faith Radio, uh people who need to catch up will be able to go back and listen to my original. introduction to the book of Romans"],[62.52,"But for folks here who can't listen to it yet, um, because uh there's some lag time."],[69.0,"I think this week they posted the beginning of Acts chapter 19."],[73.48,"So that gives you an idea of how far ahead we are."],[76.52,"So St. Paul writes his epistle, uh his letter to uh the church in Rome uh following even though he has not been there himself yet, he's still hoping to get there at this point, uh, because of some things that have happened uh under the Emperor Claudius, uh, the Jews were expelled from the city of Rome uh for about a year, and so the Gentile Christians sort of continued to have keep the church functioning while they were gone."],[108.16,"Um and then now a year later, they've come back into the uh into the city."],[116.48,"And so one of St."],[118.0,"Paul's primary concerns with the church in Rome is that with this group that's been gone now coming back in, that the church get reintegrated, that they're not end up being sort of a Jewish Christian group over here and then a Gentile Christian group over here, but that they reintegrate and they remain one and they remain united."],[140.64,"Uh, and so to that end, uh he began his epistle to the Romans uh by uh talking about the the state, sinful state of both Gentiles and Jewish people, apart from Christ, talking about how Christ came to save both, that both are saved in the same way in Christ, to position it to them that they're in the same boat from whatever background they come from."],[172.84,"And so they need to remain, uh they need to remain uh one church."],[178.04,"And so the next major section that leads up to where we're going to begin tonight, St."],[184.2,"Paul's been talking about the relationship between sin and death, uh, and how uh through Adam uh death came into the world."],[196.4,"And through death coming into the world, and through the fear of death, sin came, and sin spread to all people, and death spread to all people."],[208.32,"Because of what Adam chose to do, all human beings became mortal."],[212.88,"And he's going to talk about that a little more tonight."],[215.92,"And being mortal, all human beings fell into sin."],[220.96,"And presenting Christ as the solution to this problem in overcoming death and overcoming sin in order for our salvation."],[233.84,"And so in chapter 7, right before we're going to pick up here, he's been talking about first baptism as this transitional moment where they went from their old self in the world that's ruled by sin and death and came into Christ and so have a new life where they've been set free from sin and death."],[264.04,"And then to talk about how he talks about the flesh, and I'll talk about that a little more when we get started here."],[273.0,"But talks about the flesh, our sinful flesh, that part of us that is a part of this world, not in the sense of the physical world, but in the sense of the world of sin and death, that mortal flesh, and how that continues uh to try to drag us back into sin, and how it exploits and takes advantage of even the holy things God gives us."],[295.36,"The example he uses is the law, God's law, the Torah, uh, that hearing that rather than it producing obedience in him in his sinful flesh, it gave him occasion to go out and sin."],[311.62,"When he heard the commandments, rather than saying, okay, these are good and I will follow them, he ended up going and breaking them."],[319.14,"R rightight,? Because his flesh sort of rebelled, rebelled against them."],[324.02,"And so that's where we're gonna be picking up here uh at the beginning of uh chapter. 8, verse 1"],[331.1,"In chapter 8, here he's going to use both the terms flesh and the term body, and they're not the same thing."],[339.9,"We tend to think of them that way."],[342.22,"So when he talks about our body, he's talking about our physical body and the physical world."],[348.38,"When he talks about the flesh, he's talking about the sinful part of ourselves."],[354.22,"Okay. They're two, they're two different words."],[357.36,"It's referring to our carnal desires as opposed to the actual physical being."],[361.33,"Okay. And this is this is derived from his understanding, and the understanding both in ancient Judaism and in uh the early church in the in the uh church fathers, how they understood what happens in Genesis 3, uh, when uh Adam and Eve are expelled from the garden."],[383.07,"He's already been talking about Adam, but we're told that God uh kills some animals and then he makes them garments of flesh."],[392.27,"Now, a lot of people interpret this woodenly literally, as if God likes killed some animals to skin them and made them little suits of clothes, which isn't right, isn't what is happening there."],[404.51,"U,h the way this was interpreted in ancient Judaism by the church fathers is that uh God killed the animals to show them what death was."],[414.87,"Because he just told them that now they were going to die."],[417.79,"Okay And they had no way of knowing what that meant."],[424.01,"And it was right in front of them."],[426.07,"And the garments of skin they were given means that they received, when they came into this world, they received a different type of body."],[433.59,"And that's translated as garments of flesh."],[437.41,"And this is going to be related, it's going to be a while before we get there, but when we get to 1 Corinthians 15, and St."],[443.57,"Paul is talking about the resurrection, he's going to talk about our resurrection body."],[448.93,"That just as Christ's body, after he rose from the dead, he still had a body."],[452.94,"St. Thomas comes and touches the wounds and sees the wounds."],[456.62,"So it's not that he doesn't have a body anymore, but his body is different."],[461.12,"His body of the resurrection is different than his previous human body."],[467.6,"He has the mortal wounds but isn't dead."],[470.08,"And so in 1 Corinthians 15, he's going to talk about how our current mortal body is going to die and then be raised and transformed."],[482.72,"And he sees the opposite as having happened in Genesis 3."],[490.32,"That Adam and Eve had their original bodies that they were created with."],[494.82,"Without sin. And then when they they come into this world."],[500.6,"When they're expelled from paradise into this world, they're given bodies that fit with this world, that are subject to death and corruption and decay."],[505.28,"Okay And so that's how that's how St."],[516.6,"Paul understands. He's gonna allude to that fairly specifically later in chapter 8."],[520.84,"Um, but that's so that's the difference."],[523.32,"When he's talking about the body, he could talk about the body Adam was created with the bodies we have now, the bot resurrection body."],[532.94,"So body is not positive or negative necessarily."],[538.3,"Um, but flesh is bad."],[542.82,"It's almost considered like the fles the inversive spirit, almost."],[547.08,"Yeah, and that's exactly how he's gonna use it here."],[548.6,"Um, when he refers to spirit, he doesn't mean our spirit like our soul"],[549.52,"Okay. He means the spirit, the holy spirit who's dwelling within us and transforming us."],[561.09,"So this this is important because this is a way Saint Paul is sometimes misunderstood."],[566.93,"Uh he's not saying that like the immaterial part of us, our soul, our spirit is good, and the physical part of us, our body is bad."],[575.83,"That's the way a lot of paganism looks at it."],[579.37,"Like Plato said, the body is the prison of the soul."],[583.37,"So they thought it was a good thing when you died, because your your soul, which is your best part, was sort of set free. of your body and of this world"],[594.29,"And so that's why, like in Acts 17, when Saint Paul is preaching the gospel in Athens, and the philosophers are there with him to discuss it, they're all on board until he talks about the resurrection."],[607.42,"And then they all start laughing at him because they're like, why would you possibly want to have a body again?"],[614.64,"Like it makes no sense."],[616.24,"It makes no sense to them."],[618.48,"But that's that's not the understanding of Christianity."],[622.16,"That's not what Saint and St."],[623.52,"Paul's getting this from the fact that when Christ rose, he still has a body."],[628.1,"And that God created the world good."],[630.26,"So the creation is good."],[633.22,"It's become subject to sin through our sinfulness."],[635.22,"But the creation is like a holdover from a pagan Roman concept"],[644.86,"And this is this is why ancient Jewish and Christian burial customs are different from pagan burial customs."],[652.23,"The Romans Romans cremated everybody because your body was trash."],[657.03,"It was junk that you'd left behind and gone to a better place."],[661.29,"Whereas Christian Jews and Christians believe, no, your body is still you, and it's going to be raised up on it's going to be raised up on the last day, and so it's treated with respect."],[672.41,"This is why we treat the relics of the saints with respect."],[677.31,"And set them out because we don't think the body is bad or evil and we think it's going to be raised and transformed when Christ when Christ returns people will be reunited with it."],[689.11,"So right so when when Saint Paul is going to here in chapter 8 contrast flesh and the spirit he's talking about two principles that are at work inside of us."],[703.51,"If we've been baptized he's already talked about baptism we were chrismat received the Holy Spirit right so we have the Spirit of God."],[711.31,"We have the Holy Spirit dwelling within us, who's working with us to transform us, to free us from sin, to bring about repentance."],[720.43,"To bring us closer to Christ to trans,form us in our lives."],[723.63,"And then we also have this sinful flesh still lingering around that's trying to drag us back and pull us, pull us in the other direction."],[735.23,"And he's going to talk about the fact that we we have to live our lives according to one of those principles or the other."],[747.79,"So I think that's a good introduction to go ahead now and get into St."],[753.15,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans, uh, chapter 8, verse 1."],[758.03,"There is therefore now no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus, who do not walk according to the flesh, but according to the spirit."],[766.67,"So this is that language already"],[770.44,"There are those who choose to and walk here is a common."],[774.84,"It's all through the New Testament, but it's all through Jewish literature too."],[778.04,"Walk is referring to the way we live our life, as a whole."],[782.06,"So there are those who live according to the flesh, following its desires, and there are people who live following the Holy Spirit who dwells within them."],[793.66,"And if you follow the Holy Spirit who dwells within us, then there's no there's no uh condemnation for us before God."],[803.98,"For the law of the Spirit of life in Christ Jesus has made me free from the law of sin and death."],[810.62,"This is picking up on the law language he has already used"],[815.78,"Um we have to remember we get we get law in English from the fact that Saint Jerome translated the Greek word gnomos with the Latin word lex, and the Latin word lex means law."],[829.78,"But the Greek word gnomos and the Hebrew word Torah that it translates have a broader meaning than that."],[838.18,"So Torah in Hebrew just means teaching in a broad sense."],[844.66,"Um so it's God's teaching."],[846.5,"But gnomos in Greek includes a lot of things."],[850.18,"There were a lot of debates at this time in philosophical schools in the first century when Saint Paul is writing about what what is. is according to gnomos, what is according to law, we might say, and according to nature"],[863.5,"So for example, they would say the speed limit, is according to law."],[871.16,"There's nothing naturally wrong about driving 35 miles an hour down a street."],[877.62,"But because they've posted a sign that says speed limit 25, now it's wrong to go 35."],[884.44,"If there was no sign though, you know, you could go 35 and you wouldn't be doing anything wrong."],[890.76,"There are other things that are just wrong by nature."],[893.36,"Murder is wrong."],[896.58,"Theft. These things are just wrong by the nature of things."],[898.5,"Just by by the nature of things"],[905.28,"But so that use of gnomos, shows you that it's broader."],[909.46,"It includes not just laws, but customs, culture."],[913.8,"All these a way of life, a way of living."],[916.6,"Okay. So when St."],[919.48,"Paul uses that term, when he talks about the law of the spirit of life and the law of sin and death, he's not talking about like two lists of commandments."],[929.73,"He's talking about a whole way of life, of sin that leads to death, and of the spirit that leads to life."],[940.65,"So it's like there's these two paths that you can go down to continue his walking language"],[947.49,"You can walk down this path following the spirit that will lead you to life."],[951.89,"You can walk down this other path of sin that's going to lead you to death."],[959.89,"And so because he now has the spirit dwelling within him, because of Jesus Christ, he has the spirit dwelling within him, and that has set him free from that path of sin leading to death that he was on before."],[976.13,"He was walking that path."],[978.23,"Christ by giving him his spirit has now set him free from that to walk the path that leads to life."],[985.19,"For what the law could not do in that it was weak through the flesh, God did by sending his own son in the likeness of sinful flesh on account of sin."],[995.79,"He condemned sin in the flesh."],[998.43,"I'll pause there because there's a ton there."],[1018.27,"And here he's when he says the law like this, he's talking about the Torah."],[1022.35,"He's talking about the Old Testament Jewish law."],[1025.44,"And he's made the point about what it was for."],[1029.17,"What was its purpose?"],[1031.57,"And its purpose was that it was put into place to sort of manage sin until Christ came to deal with it."],[1042.45,"So there's this problem in the world that there's sin, there's wickedness, there's death."],[1046.95,"There's all this destruction."],[1048.71,"That's gotta be."],[1050.48,"Christ was going to come and deal with it."],[1053.46,"And deal with sin."],[1054.9,"Until that point, it had to be managed."],[1058.98,"And so this system was put in place in the first five books of the Bible in the Torah, whereby there were sin offerings and sacrifices and ways of expressing repentance, repenting and being cleansed of sin."],[1073.0,"But this had to happen over and over and over and over and over again."],[1077.08,"Because it wasn't really dealing with the problem."],[1080.28,"It wasn't transforming people."],[1082.78,"It was just sort of managing the issue."],[1086.88,"Keeping all the plates spinning."],[1090.0,"And the reason the reason it couldn't change people is because people with their sinful flesh weren't capable of just sort of changing by themselves."],[1103.37,"It wasn't a question of, well, if I just try hard enough, I could stop sinning."],[1107.85,"It doesn't work that way."],[1108.97,"God had to intervene."],[1109.93,"And so this cycle just kept repeating itself."],[1112.97,"And we see that all through the Old Testament, not only in the Torah, but in the history of Israel."],[1117.81,"Where again and again they fall into sin."],[1120.69,"God sends someone."],[1122.55,"Some oppressor. Ultimately they end up going into exile."],[1125.91,"They repent, they're forgiven, God restores them."],[1129.03,"But the cycle just keeps happening over and over, and they fall back into sin."],[1132.89,"It keeps going on over and over and over again."],[1135.75,"Because people are weak."],[1138.63,"They're weak in their flesh and they're prone to sin."],[1141.59,"And so God has to intervene to stop, to stop that cycle."],[1146.23,"Just giving the management system isn't enough."],[1150.97,"To stop, to stop this cycle from happening."],[1154.65,"Over and over."],[1155.77,"And this is one of the themes in the passages in Joel, the other prophetic books in the Old Testament that prophesy about the new covenant in Jeremiah 31 that prophesy about the fact that the Holy Spirit is going to come and is going to dwell within people."],[1170.53,"This is what God says."],[1172.93,"God says, you know, I gave you my law."],[1175.49,"I told you, I gave you the commandments, I told you to keep them, and you couldn't keep them."],[1180.54,"He says, So now I'm going to put my spirit in you, and my spirit will complete them."],[1186.7,"My spirit will keep them."],[1189.4,"So St. Paul is drawing on that idea too to say this is now being fulfilled."],[1196.1,"What the law couldn't do, what just giving the commandments couldn't do is now happening."],[1201.88,"Through the power of the Holy Spirit."],[1202.36,"And the Holy Spirit came because of what Christ did"],[1207.6,"So he says, God did it, but the law couldn't do, by sending his own son in the likeness of sinful flesh, on account of sin."],[1217.2,"Okay. So why does it say the likeness of sinful flesh?"],[1221.04,"Well, Christ had a body like ours."],[1223.78,"But he didn't have sin."],[1226.98,"Christ himself was not sinful."],[1229.62,"But he still had a body like ours."],[1232.31,"And we're told he was tempted, the devil comes and tempts him."],[1235.67,"He gets tired."],[1236.95,"He gets weak."],[1239.13,"He gets weary."],[1240.73,"Because he has a body like ours."],[1242.49,"So that's what it means by the likeness of sinful flesh."],[1244.97,"Now notice it's the the likeness of sinful flesh, not likeness of a body."],[1250.66,"Christ really has a body for Saint Paul."],[1255.06,"It's that he doesn't have this flesh element that we have."],[1261.52,"And he does this."],[1264.56,"Uh what it actually a better translation of that from the Greek would be he condemned sin in this flesh, meaning in Christ's flesh."],[1274.37,"God condemned condemns sin in Christ's flesh."],[1278.77,"And Saint Paul is here uh alluding to uh the ritual that was done on the day of atonement, which is Yom Kippur in Hebrew."],[1288.37,"On the Day of Atonement, once a year, in addition to the sin offerings that were offered all the time"],[1295.61,"When people sinned, they would come, they'd bring an animal, they confess their sin, the animal would be sacrificed, they'd eat it."],[1303.29,"And uh serious forgiveness of sins."],[1307.13,"Once a year, uh there was another ritual that was done to cleanse the people and the camp of sin."],[1316.81,"This shows you once again the issue with with the Torah with the law."],[1320.9,"Even doing all these sin offerings wasn't enough."],[1324.0,"We still have because the way it's set up is um to boil it down a little, is that there was sort of this residue."],[1332.16,"Of sin that all that sin left"],[1336.32,"Even as people sinned and they were forgiven, there was just sort of this taint of this residue in the camp itself."],[1342.74,"And later in the in the city and in the nation itself that had to be gotten rid of."],[1348.26,"I've said this before in Romans, but we t we tend to look at sin from a legal perspective."],[1354.18,"We think about it like God gives a commitment."],[1355.86,"God makes a rule and you break the rule, so now you're guilty of this crime, and God forgiving you is letting you off the hook."],[1363.3,"It's a category"],[1368.02,"And no one involved with the writing of the scriptures, and no one in the early church looked at sin that way."],[1378.46,"It's called, it's called by scholars the biological view of sin."],[1382.62,"Sin was looked at as a contaminant."],[1385.9,"It was looked at like a disease."],[1389.14,"And it contaminated you, it wounded you."],[1394.66,"Sin led to death in the way that a malignant illness leads to death if it's left untreated."],[1403.48,"And so it had to be quarantined."],[1406.6,"It had to be kept separate."],[1408.84,"Okay. And so you'll find all these, you'll find all these passages in the Old Testament where they go, yeah, where things are unclean, where they go and they burn things, and you're like, why are they burning it because of the sin that was committed there?"],[1422.26,"They believed it left like this contamination."],[1426.52,"In the way that we would if there was an outbreak of plague or something."],[1431.32,"You know, we go and burn all the linens, burn all the, you know, that might be infected with it."],[1436.12,"It's a very dire view of sin."],[1437.74,"So that that's the view of sin."],[1439.5,"So someone who is this is why sin and healing is so associated in the gospels."],[1444.7,"You see Jesus healing people and forgiving their sins at the same time."],[1449.18,"And in the book of James, when it talks about unction."],[1451.83,"Anointing with oil, like we did during Holy Week, it says they will be healed of their illnesses and their sins will be forgiven."],[1458.73,"These ideas are tied together."],[1461.39,"Of sin as a spiritual illness."],[1462.83,"Okay It's a spiritual infection or a spiritual cancer that will ultimately be deadly."],[1469.83,"And so even though individual people had their sins forgiven through these sin offerings and guilt offerings, there still had to be this Day of Atonement ritual to sort of decontaminate everything."],[1482.31,"And it had two pieces."],[1483.67,"There were two goats that were taken."],[1486.23,"Uh, and they cast lots over the two goats."],[1489.11,"One of the two goats, the high priest would lay his hands on and pronounce the sins of the people."],[1497.43,"Okay, and those would be put on the into the goat."],[1501.13,"And then that goat would be driven out of the out of the camp into the wilderness. presumably to die"],[1511.57,"And then the other goat that did not have the sins on it."],[1515.51,"That was still pure."],[1517.12,"They would take they would sacrifice that goat and they would take the blood and they would sprinkle that blood on everything in the sanctuary to purify it."],[1526.72,"From the effects of sin."],[1530.4,"A lot of imagery, not just here, but in the gospels, especially in St."],[1535.2,"Matthew's gospel, of that goat that's driven out is attached to Christ."],[1541.84,"We have, in, for example, in the Epistle of Barnabas, we have various accounts from the first century and and the preceding couple of centuries and later couple of centuries about the ritual, how it was actually done of the Day of Atonement at this time in history."],[1559.32,"And when you read the details, like of the Epistle of Barnabas, that they would take reeds and beat the goat with it to drive it out of the city."],[1566.46,"And then you see in the story of Christ's Passion where they take the reed and strike him with it."],[1571.74,"They would drive it out, spit on the goat."],[1574.62,"It was, it was driven out."],[1576.3,"Christ sort of carrying his cross outside the city."],[1581.26,"And uh the Epistle of Hebrews makes the point that Christ dies outside the city."],[1585.84,"Is using that imagery to show us the way Christ takes upon himself the sin of the people and takes them out, takes it away and dies and takes it away forever."],[1595.52,"And so this is what St"],[1599.06,"Paul is alluding to here when he says that God condemned sin in Christ's flesh."],[1604.79,"Condemned it to death."],[1606.63,"Christ came in the in the likeness of sinful flesh, so that he could take our sin upon himself and die with it and take it away."],[1617.56,"So God the Father doesn't condemn Christ, he condemns our sin in Christ's flesh."],[1624.04,"Okay. So it's not that the goat was evil."],[1627.0,"It's the sins that were put on the goat."],[1629.4,"I can see that being taken away."],[1634.26,"So that's that's what he's that's what he's alluding to."],[1637.46,"Okay Verse 4, so that the righteous requirement of the law might be fulfilled in us who do not walk according to the flesh, but according to the spirit."],[1652.06,"So with our sin taken away, with the sin taken away, that disease, that infection taken away."],[1660.65,"It's not just taking away the guilt for things we did before."],[1663.79,"But taking away that infection, taking away that malignancy."],[1667.23,"That now frees us to walk not according to the flesh, but according to the spirit."],[1675.95,"For those who live according to the flesh set their minds on the things of the flesh, but those who live according to the sp their spitirit. the things of"],[1686.47,"So what are the things of the flesh?"],[1689.67,"Well, the flesh has a whole lot of desires."],[1694.76,"And this is related to, again, this is related to our mortality."],[1700.68,"So we can start with food, drink, comfort, but we can add in sex."],[1708.67,"We can add in all these that our status, wealth, power, that our flesh desires, and desires primarily in reaction to our mortality."],[1719.79,"We see these things, wealth, status, power, having children who are carrying on our family name after us, all in terms of trying to sort of fight against our death, or when confronted with death, we decide, well, eat, drink, and be merry because for tomorrow we'll die."],[1736.4,"So we just pursue pleasure."],[1738.64,"Okay, in this life."],[1740.32,"And so our flesh sort of craves and wants these things."],[1744.64,"And so a person who the word for mind there is fronema."],[1750.4,"It means consideration or reckoning or thinking."],[1754.24,"If you're walking according to the flesh, you spend all your time thinking about how am I going to gratify these desires?"],[1760.1,"How am I going to get more money?"],[1762.2,"How am I going to going to uh increase my status?"],[1766.86,"How am I going to that's what your concern is all the time."],[1771.06,"He says, but if you're walking according to the flesh, then your mind, your thinking is going to be concerned with the things of the, if you're walking according to the spirit, with the things of the spirit, of the Holy Spirit, the things of God."],[1781.86,"Those are gonna be the things that you're always thinking about and trying to pursue."],[1786.9,"Now it has here in the Orthodox sorry about carnally minded, but it's what it says is to have the mind of the flesh is death."],[1796.42,"But to have the mind of the spirit is life and peace."],[1799.64,"Because that's where it leads."],[1801.88,"You're not, no one sets out and says, you know, I'd really like death."],[1806.21,"I'd really like condemnation."],[1808.71,"But what he's saying is when your concern is for those things of this world, that's where it leads."],[1813.47,"That's what you get."],[1815.55,"And if you're the spirit, then that's how you receive life and peace, not by pursuing these other things."],[1822.59,"Because the mind of the flesh."],[1828.35,"The mind of the flesh is the is the enemy of God, is what it literally says."],[1833.87,"For it is not subject to the law of God, nor indeed can be."],[1842.23,"Mind if your thoughts are constantly on how do I get the next things?"],[1846.57,"How do I get more stuff?"],[1847.85,"How do I satisfy all my desires as much as possible and have as much pleasure as possible?"],[1853.31,"That can't be subject to the law of God."],[1856.61,"You're going to run up against it all the time."],[1859.73,"All the time."],[1860.53,"The things that you want are going to be denied you by God's law."],[1867.49,"And so that means you're always going to be an enemy."],[1871.49,"You're always going to end up being an enemy of God."],[1874.45,"Because his law is constantly going to be telling you that you can't have or you shouldn't have all these things that you want and that you're chasing after."],[1882.69,"It's not just a choice with consequences only to you."],[1887.98,"But you've actually set yourself up as an enemy of God by doing that."],[1894.14,"And because of that, he says in verse 8, so then those who are in the flesh cannot please God."],[1900.02,"It's impossible for anything you do to be pleasing to God if all you're pursuing is satisfying your own desires."],[1908.74,"But you are not in the flesh, but in the spirit, if indeed the spirit of God dwells in you."],[1914.74,"Now, if anyone does not have the spirit of Christ, he is not his."],[1930.46,"We, as Christians have received the Holy Spirit, and so we're not to be chasing after the things of the flesh."],[1938.3,"We have the Spirit, and the Spirit makes it possible for us to please God."],[1943.66,"Does it mean that everything we do is automatically pleasing to God?"],[1947.12,"Remember, he's been talking about freedom."],[1949.12,"We're now free from chasing after the things of this world."],[1953.52,"We're free from the flesh, we're free from sin."],[1955.84,"Free to, if we desire to, now pursue the things of God and to please God."],[1962.16,"He says, now if anyone does not have the spirit of Christ, he is not his, because this is the marker."],[1967.38,"That we belong to Christ."],[1968.82,"So we've been baptized into Christ and we've received the Holy Spirit."],[1972.98,"And if Christ is in you, the body is dead because of sin, but the spirit is life because of righteousness."],[1979.38,"Now notice he switched, he switched here from body to flesh."],[1984.18,"Let me read one more verse."],[1985.14,"But if the spirit of him who raised Jesus from the dead dwells in you, he who raised Christ from the dead will also give life to your mortal bodies through his spirit who dwells in you."],[1995.9,"So he switches from flesh to body."],[1997.98,"He's already covered that we're not a slave to the flesh anymore."],[2001.74,"But now what he's saying is your body, your old self is dead."],[2006.88,"It's dead. But the Holy Spirit is living within you, and that means that your yourself who is dead is going to be raised from the dead in the same way."],[2017.84,"In the same way that Christ is raised from the dead."],[2020.96,"Because it was the same spirit who raised him."],[2026.72,"It's interesting to note, as an aside, all of the Trinitarian language all through here"],[2033.64,"Oh, yeah, there's definitely so he's already said several times that God raised Christ from the dead, meaning the Father, and now he says the Spirit raised Christ from the dead."],[2041.8,"So who does that mean the Spirit is?"],[2044.89,"So all through here, the interrelationship of all three persons of the Trinity."],[2050.73,"So what goes under the Spirit?"],[2052.97,"Like, I know it's like the completely, it's the opposite of the of the flesh of the sin, but what goes under the Yeah."],[2060.65,"Well, in full in Philippians, St."],[2062.89,"Paul lays out the fruit of the spirit and then the works of the flesh."],[2067.13,"He lists a bunch of them."],[2068.73,"And the works of the flesh, we already says the fruits of the spirit are love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness.ness, faithful, gentleness, self-control, all of these things"],[2082.53,"And he ends that list by saying, against these things there is no law."],[2086.71,"Because remember, he's talking about how the spirit fulfills the law within us."],[2091.03,"So if the spirit is producing these fruit in us, if we have love and joy and peace and all of these things going on in us, we'll we'll keep the commandments."],[2101.67,"Don't have to worry about following a whole list of rules."],[2104.33,"If we're doing all of those things."],[2106.09,"And this is part of what's going on in the gospels and the conflict all the time between Christ and the Pharisees."],[2112.41,"You know, where Christ heals someone, does something to show love and gentleness and kindness to someone, and they're like, Well, why'd you do that on the Sabbath?"],[2119.53,"There's a rule."],[2120.49,"Because they don't they don't understand"],[2126.35,"They don't have the Spirit of God, they don't have those things."],[2129.87,"So believing that the law can say them."],[2132.01,"They're trying to use the law to do something it's powerless to do."],[2137.69,"And you have to keep the entire law."],[2139.85,"Yeah. In other words."],[2143.13,"Yeah. So whenever it says please God, it's referring to those fruit of the fruit of the spirit that you're saying."],[2153.78,"God produces them in us, and they please him because they're his doing."],[2160.28,"In our lives."],[2161.08,"So just as God looks at when he's creating the world in Genesis 1. he creates something and he looks at it and he sees that it is good"],[2168.56,"As the Holy Spirit brings forth these things in our life, God looks at them and he says that they're good."],[2174.48,"But I mean the Holy Spirit puts these things, but you have to contribute them."],[2178.27,"And we're gonna, he's gonna he's gonna get into that here with some of the some of the language he used."],[2183.23,"Yeah, he said all the way through, remember, he's been saying there's a choice."],[2186.05,"You can walk according to the flesh or you could walk according to the spirit."],[2190.37,"We're now and he says we're now free to walk according to the spirit."],[2193.73,"But that fact that we're free to do that means we have to choose to do it."],[2200.29,"Not so the Holy Spirit can be"],[2205.81,"Well, I mean, Psalm 50 says yes."],[2207.97,"es. Uh and there are passag not here that talk about grieving the Holy Spirit, quenching the Holy Spirit"],[2216.25,"So, yeah, it's it's actually worse."],[2224.62,"Because if we have the Holy Spirit dwelling in us, and St."],[2227.82,"Paul's going to get into this, if we have the Holy Spirit dwelling within us, then that makes the the sins and the wicked things we choose to do worse because we're sort of doing them in his presence."],[2240.06,"Oh, so they cannot be doing it."],[2241.48,"It's like, yeah, it's like it's like it's like doing them in church."],[2245.54,"Though God is if God is dwelling within us, he says, for example, at one point, apparently they're having problems with. sexual immorality"],[2254.22,"And he says, you know, you're part of Christ's body, are you going to go join the members of Christ to a prostitute?"],[2260.47,"Like that's the language he uses."],[2263.13,"So it's actually worse for us."],[2265.21,"That's why he talks about, in terms of receiving communion, in an unworthy way."],[2270.25,"He doesn't say if you're unworthy to receive communion."],[2273.29,"It's an adverb, not an adjective."],[2275.29,"He says receiving it in an unworthy way."],[2277.93,"The way that we come to receive is the problem."],[2282.83,"If we don't come with repentance, then that's worse than if we don't receive communion."],[2291.23,"And so it's it's the same kind of thing."],[2293.23,"Having the Holy Spirit with us raises the stakes."],[2295.79,"And we talked about that back in Acts"],[2298.87,"Remember, the Holy Spirit comes onto the people, and then there's those two people, Ananias and Sapphira."],[2305.03,"And they come, they sold some property, and they come and they bring some part of the money to give it to the apostles."],[2313.51,"And keep some for themselves."],[2315.59,"And that's not the problem."],[2318.15,"The problem is St."],[2319.43,"Peter, when he receives the money, says, Is this all the money that you received?"],[2323.35,"And they say, Yeah."],[2328.15,"And they fall over dead."],[2331.99,"And Saint Peter says, you know, it was your money to do whatever you wanted with"],[2338.49,"He said, but you came here and you lied."],[2339.85,"you And didn't lie to a group of men."],[2340.09,"lied. You to the Holy Spirit"],[2347.47,"You lied to God."],[2351.16,"So the Holy Spirit being present with us sort of raises those, raises those stakes."],[2358.12,"Because, and and Saint Paul talked about this at the beginning of Romans as well, when he was comparing the Jews and the Gentiles."],[2366.6,"When he was saying the Gentiles, you know, they didn't they didn't receive the Torah."],[2370.36,"They didn't receive God's commandments and this kind of thing."],[2373.48,"And they went and they sinned, sure."],[2375.56,"But then when he's talking to the Jewish people in the Christian community, he says, You guys had it."],[2380.52,"You guys had the commandment."],[2381.8,"And you still went and sinned."],[2384.36,"So that makes you more accountable."],[2386.88,"Because you should have known they didn't have any way of knowing."],[2389.2,"You should have known better."],[2392.08,"And so that's one of St."],[2394.0,"Paul's themes here is God holds us accountable for what He's given, whatever He's given us, He holds us accountable for what we do with it."],[2401.84,"And so the more He's given us, the more accountable we are."],[2415.16,"Wealthy, whose God has given them all these blessings of wealth, he's going to hold them accountable for what they did with that wealth."],[2421.64,"For people who have been given a lot of knowledge of God and the Christian faith."],[2428.94,"God's going to hold them to a higher level than somebody who, you know, is illiterate and you know doesn't know much, but sort of does the best they can, you know, with what they know."],[2439.66,"So yeah, that's that's part of this."],[2441.9,"Is now that we've received the Spirit and we're now free to follow the Spirit and to please God, if we choose to not do that, if we choose to go back to living the way we were, then things will be worse for us than if we had never received those things."],[2460.22,"So not a cheery note."],[2466.54,"But sort of the other side."],[2469.66,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2474.14,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecouncil at ancientfaithcom."],[2482.42,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":26.4,"lv":2,"h":"Catch-up: why Romans was written","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up <b>Romans 8</b>; ~2 months since last Bible study</li><li class=\"d1\">intro to Romans on <b>Ancient Faith</b> for those who need to catch up (they've just posted Acts 19)</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul writes church in Rome - <b>hasn't been there yet</b>, hopes to go</li><li class=\"d1\">under <b>Emperor Claudius</b> the <b>Jews expelled</b> from Rome ~a year</li><li class=\"d1\">Gentile Christians kept the church functioning; now Jews <b>back in the city</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's concern: <b>reintegrate</b> - not a Jewish Christian group here and a Gentile one there, but <b>one and united</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So he opens with the <b>sinful state of both</b> Gentiles and Jews apart from Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ came to save both, <b>the same way</b> - \"same boat\"</li></ul>"},{"t":178.04,"lv":3,"h":"Where the argument stands: sin, death, flesh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Sin and death: through <b>Adam</b> death entered the world</li><li class=\"d1\">through death and <b>fear of death</b> came sin; spread to all</li><li class=\"d1\">Adam's choice → all human beings <b>mortal</b> → mortal, all fell into sin</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ</b> = solution, overcoming death and sin</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ch. 7</b>: baptism as transitional moment - out of old self ruled by sin and death → <b>set free</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>The flesh</b> = part of us belonging to the world of <b>sin and death</b> - not the physical world</li><li class=\"d1\">keeps trying to drag us back; <b>exploits even holy things</b></li><li class=\"d1\">example: the <b>Torah</b> - commandment gave occasion to sin; his flesh <b>rebelled</b></li></ul>"},{"t":331.1,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 8 - Flesh vs body; Genesis 3 garments","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 8 uses <b>flesh</b> and <b>body</b> - <b>not the same thing</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>body</b> = physical body, physical world</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>flesh</b> = the <b>sinful part</b>, carnal desires</li><li class=\"d0\">Roots: <b>Genesis 3</b> - God kills animals, makes <b>garments of skin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"little suits of clothes\" - wooden literalism</li><li class=\"d1\">ancient Jewish and Fathers' reading: God showed them <b>what death was</b> - they'd no way of knowing</li><li class=\"d1\">garments = a <b>different type of body</b> on entering this world (\"garments of flesh\")</li></ul>"},{"t":437.41,"lv":3,"h":"Resurrection body; Adam's two bodies","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Preview: <b>1 Corinthians 15</b> - our resurrection body</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ after rising still has a body - <b>Thomas touches the wounds</b></li><li class=\"d1\">body of the resurrection <b>different</b>: mortal wounds but not dead</li><li class=\"d1\">our mortal body dies, is <b>raised and transformed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Opposite happened in Genesis 3: Adam and Eve had <b>original bodies</b>, without sin</li><li class=\"d1\">expelled → bodies <b>fit for this world</b>, subject to <b>death, corruption, decay</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul alludes to this later in ch. 8</li><li class=\"d0\">So <b>body</b> not positive or negative in itself - Adam's body, ours now, resurrection body</li></ul>"},{"t":538.3,"lv":2,"h":"Flesh is bad; \"Spirit\" = Holy Spirit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Flesh</b> is bad - nearly the inverse of the spirit</li><li class=\"d0\">When he says <b>spirit</b> - not our soul but the <b>Holy Spirit</b> dwelling in us, transforming us</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul often <b>misunderstood</b>: not \"soul good, body bad\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that's <b>paganism</b> - <b>Plato</b>: \"body is the prison of the soul\"; death sets the best part free</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 17</b>, Athens: philosophers on board until the <b>resurrection</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then laughter: \"why would you want a body again?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Christianity: Christ rose with a body; <b>God created the world good</b></li><li class=\"d1\">creation became subject to sin <b>through our sinfulness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Burial customs: Romans <b>cremated</b> - body was trash, junk left behind</li><li class=\"d1\">Jews and Christians: body <b>is still you</b>, raised on the last day → treated with respect</li><li class=\"d1\">why we treat <b>relics of the saints</b> with respect</li></ul>"},{"t":689.11,"lv":3,"h":"Two principles at work in us","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Flesh and spirit = <b>two principles</b> inside us</li><li class=\"d1\">baptized, chrismated → <b>Holy Spirit</b> dwelling in us</li><li class=\"d1\">Spirit works to <b>transform, free from sin, bring repentance</b>, bring us closer to Christ</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sinful flesh</b> still lingering, pulling the other way</li><li class=\"d0\">We <b>live by one or the other</b></li></ul>"},{"t":747.79,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 8:1-2 - No condemnation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:1</b> \"no condemnation to those in Christ Jesus who do not <b>walk</b> according to the flesh but the spirit\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>walk</b> = how we live our life as a whole - all through NT and Jewish literature</li><li class=\"d1\">follow the Holy Spirit within → <b>no condemnation</b> before God</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:2</b> \"law of the Spirit of life… free from the law of sin and death\"</li><li class=\"d1\">picks up the <b>law</b> language of ch. 7</li></ul>"},{"t":815.78,"lv":3,"h":"What \"law\" means: nomos","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">English \"law\" - <b>Jerome</b> translated Greek <i>nomos</i> with Latin <i>lex</i></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek <i>nomos</i> and Hebrew <i>Torah</i> broader - Torah = <b>teaching</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First-c. philosophical schools debated: by <b>law</b> vs by <b>nature</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>speed limit</b>: 35 mph isn't naturally wrong - the sign makes it wrong</li><li class=\"d1\">murder, theft = wrong <b>by nature</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Nomos</i> includes laws, <b>customs, culture</b>, a way of life</li><li class=\"d0\">So \"law of the Spirit of life\" and \"law of sin and death\" = not two lists of commandments but <b>two ways of life</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>two paths</b> (walking language): Spirit → life, sin → death</li><li class=\"d1\">Spirit within, from Jesus, has <b>set him free</b> from the path he was on</li></ul>"},{"t":985.19,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 8:3 - What the law could not do","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:3</b> \"what the law could not do in that it was weak through the flesh, God did by sending his own Son… on account of sin\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Law = <b>Torah</b>; its purpose: <b>manage sin until Christ came</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sin offerings, sacrifices, repentance and cleansing - <b>over and over and over</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"keeping all the plates spinning\"; <b>not transforming</b> people</li><li class=\"d0\">Couldn't change them: sinful flesh - not \"if I try hard enough I could stop sinning\"; <b>God had to intervene</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Cycle all through OT history: sin → oppressor → <b>exile</b> → repent, forgiven, restored → sin again</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Prophecy</b>: Joel, <b>Jeremiah 31</b> - the Holy Spirit will dwell within people</li><li class=\"d1\">God: \"I gave you my law… you couldn't keep them… now I'll <b>put my Spirit in you</b>, my Spirit will keep them\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: now being <b>fulfilled</b> through the Holy Spirit, who came because of Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":1207.6,"lv":3,"h":"\"Likeness of sinful flesh\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why <b>likeness</b> of sinful flesh? Christ had a body <b>like ours</b>, but <b>no sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tempted by the devil; gets <b>tired, weak, weary</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not likeness of a <b>body</b> - Christ really has a body; he lacks the <b>flesh element</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Better Greek: God condemned sin <b>in this flesh</b> = in Christ's flesh</li><li class=\"d1\">alluding to <b>Day of Atonement</b> (<i>Yom Kippur</i>)</li></ul>"},{"t":1288.37,"lv":2,"h":"Day of Atonement and the \"biological\" view of sin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ordinary sin offerings: bring animal, <b>confess</b>, sacrificed, eat it → forgiveness</li><li class=\"d0\">Once a year another ritual to <b>cleanse people and camp</b> - shows the limit of the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">sin left a <b>residue</b>, taint in camp, city, nation</li><li class=\"d0\">We read sin <b>legally</b>: God makes a rule, you break it, guilty, forgiveness = off the hook</li><li class=\"d1\">no one in Scripture or early church saw it so</li><li class=\"d0\">Scholars: the <b>biological view of sin</b> - sin as <b>contaminant, disease, wound</b></li><li class=\"d1\">leads to death like <b>untreated malignant illness</b>; must be <b>quarantined</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT passages burning unclean things - like burning linens in a plague</li><li class=\"d0\">Why <b>sin and healing</b> go together: Jesus heals and forgives at once; <b>James</b> - unction, healed and sins forgiven</li><li class=\"d1\">\"spiritual infection or spiritual cancer\", ultimately deadly</li></ul>"},{"t":1482.31,"lv":3,"h":"The two goats; Christ as the goat","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Two goats</b>, lots cast</li><li class=\"d1\">one: high priest lays hands, <b>pronounces the sins</b> → driven out into the wilderness, presumably to die</li><li class=\"d1\">other, still pure: <b>sacrificed</b>, blood sprinkled on all in the sanctuary to <b>purify</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel imagery, esp. <b>Matthew</b>, attaches the driven-out goat to Christ</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Epistle of Barnabas</b> + 1st-c. accounts: goat <b>beaten with reeds</b>, spat on, driven out of the city</li><li class=\"d1\">Passion: <b>reed</b> strikes Christ; carries cross outside the city; <b>Hebrews</b>: dies outside the city</li><li class=\"d0\">So 8:3: God <b>condemned sin in Christ's flesh</b> - to death</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ takes our sin, <b>dies with it, takes it away</b> forever</li><li class=\"d1\">Father <b>doesn't condemn Christ</b> - condemns our sin; \"not that the goat was evil\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1637.46,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 8:4-6 - Mind of flesh vs mind of Spirit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:4</b> righteous requirement of the law fulfilled in us who walk by the Spirit</li><li class=\"d1\">sin taken away - not just guilt for past deeds but the <b>infection, malignancy</b> → free to walk by the Spirit</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:5</b> things of the flesh: <b>food, drink, comfort, sex, status, wealth, power</b>, children carrying the name</li><li class=\"d1\">flesh desires mainly in <b>reaction to our mortality</b> - or \"eat, drink, be merry\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Mind\" = <i>phronema</i> = <b>reckoning, thinking</b></li><li class=\"d1\">flesh: always thinking how to <b>gratify, get more money, raise status</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Spirit: thinking and pursuing <b>things of God</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:6</b> mind of flesh = <b>death</b>; mind of Spirit = <b>life and peace</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no one wants death or condemnation - <b>that's where it leads</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1828.35,"lv":3,"h":"8:7-8 - Enemy of God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:7</b> mind of the flesh = <b>enemy of God</b>, not subject to God's law, nor can be</li><li class=\"d1\">pursuing all desires → you'll run into the law <b>all the time</b>; denied what you want</li><li class=\"d1\">you set yourself up as <b>God's enemy</b> - not a choice with consequences only to you</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:8</b> in the flesh <b>cannot please God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">nothing pleasing if all you pursue is <b>satisfying your desires</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1908.74,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 8:9-11 - The Spirit dwells in you","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:9</b> not in the flesh but in the Spirit <b>if the Spirit of God dwells in you</b>; no Spirit of Christ = <b>not his</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christians have the Spirit - not to chase the flesh; Spirit makes it <b>possible to please God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not automatically pleasing - <b>freedom</b>: free to pursue God <b>if we desire to</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>marker</b> that we belong to Christ: baptized, received the Spirit</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:10</b> \"body is dead because of sin, spirit is life\" - switches from flesh to <b>body</b></li><li class=\"d1\">flesh already covered; now: <b>old self dead</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:11</b> Spirit of him who raised Jesus dwells in you → will <b>give life to your mortal bodies</b></li><li class=\"d1\">raised the same way - same Spirit raised Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: <b>Trinitarian language</b> throughout</li><li class=\"d1\">Father raised Christ - now the <b>Spirit</b> raised Christ; all three persons interrelated</li></ul>"},{"t":2052.97,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: What goes under the Spirit?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: what falls under \"the Spirit\" vs flesh?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Galatians</b> (said Philippians) - <b>fruit of the Spirit</b> then works of the flesh</li><li class=\"d1\">love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, <b>self-control</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"against these things there is <b>no law</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Spirit <b>fulfills the law within us</b> - no need to follow a list of rules</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospels: <b>Christ vs the Pharisees</b> - heals on the Sabbath; \"there's a rule\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they lack the Spirit's fruit; <b>using law</b> for what it's powerless to do</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: \"please God\" = those fruits?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God produces them in us</b>, pleasing because they're his doing</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Genesis 1</b> - sees it and calls it good</li></ul>"},{"t":2174.48,"lv":3,"h":"Q: Our choice; grieving the Spirit; higher stakes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Spirit puts it in, but <b>you contribute</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">all along a <b>choice</b> - walk by flesh or Spirit; free to walk by Spirit means <b>we must choose</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: can the Spirit be... - <b>Psalm 50</b> yes; passages on <b>grieving, quenching</b> the Spirit</li><li class=\"d0\">Actually <b>worse</b>: sins done in <b>his presence</b> - \"like doing them in church\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1 Corinthians</b>: \"join the members of Christ to a prostitute?\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>communion</b>: \"in an unworthy <b>way</b>\" - adverb, not adjective; coming <b>without repentance</b> is the problem</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 5</b>: <b>Ananias and Sapphira</b> sold property, kept some back, <b>fell dead</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Peter: money was yours to do with as you wished - \"you lied to the Holy Spirit… <b>to God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Having the Holy Spirit with us <b>raises the stakes</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2358.12,"lv":3,"h":"Accountability for what we are given","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Beginning of Romans: Gentiles sinned without the Torah; <b>Jews had the commandment</b> → <b>more accountable</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's theme: God holds us accountable for <b>whatever he's given</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more given → <b>more accountable</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>wealthy</b> - accountable for what they did with it</li><li class=\"d1\">much <b>knowledge of God and the faith</b> → higher level than the illiterate who does the best they can</li><li class=\"d0\">Received the Spirit and free to follow - if we <b>go back to how we lived</b>, worse for us than if we'd never received it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not a cheery note\"</li></ul>"}]});
